he findings highlight the efficacy of the new rule set, while also exposing a need for careful examination of the \"needing review\" items.\n\n\nDirect matching of several items has been observed based on quantity and OCR accuracy. Specifically, ampoule counts and common drug names are successfully linked across different entries. The \"needs review\" sheet, containing 33 items, is now activated to further assess the remaining discrepancies.\n\n\nIdentified a set of 33 cases now categorized into three distinct groups based on the effectiveness of the safety net system. The first category, \"True OCR/Genuine Hits,\" comprises pharmaceutical items rescued from being marked as \"not found\" due to OCR misreads. These were confirmed through analysis of brand names, similar formulations, and specific drug identification. Each hit suggests the OCR was flawed but correctable through manual review.\n\n\nAnalyzed several specific item pairings flagged for review. Identified instances of OCR errors causing discrepancies, such as letter substitutions and omissions. Noted that in one case, a quantity difference triggered a review despite being from the same brand.\n\nAlso detected that a 'review' categorization appeared incorrect because an OCR error and a dose variant of the same brand had incorrectly been classified as a potential mismatch when it should have been a match. The review category can be refined to be more accurate in the future.\n\n\nExamining cases flagged for pharmacist review revealed clear instances of mismatching drug classes (e.g., antibiotics vs. herbal supplements). Observed that the system correctly identifies and flags pairs like `بروتولوك` and `كونترولوك` as needing review due to similar active ingredients but different brand names. The system successfully identified two items from the `المكرومي المنصورة.pdf` warehouse that were incorrectly matched and that also needed to be sent for review.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
